# Where does a price come from if nobody sets it?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How do buyers and sellers who never meet end up at one price? Why do shortages and surpluses push the price back?"
next: {"title":"What happens when demand rises or supply falls?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/08-what-happens-when-demand-rises-or-supply.cast"}

## Cards in a classroom
sources:
    - id: smith1962
      title: An Experimental Study of Competitive Market Behavior
      authors: Vernon L. Smith
      year: 1962
      finding: In classroom double auctions begun at Purdue in January 1956, traders with secret buyer and seller values converged quickly toward the competitive equilibrium price.

