    box b2 "$7" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box b3 "$6" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box b4 "$5" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box b5 "$4" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" style.stroke #b5482e in buyers hidden true
    box s1 "$2" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    box s2 "$3" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    box s3 "$4" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    box s4 "$5" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    box s5 "$6" width 140 height 130 radius 10 icon "fishing pole" style.stroke #2f6b8f in sellers hidden true
    text buyers_h "Buyers" #b5482e x 95 y 557 font_size 26 hidden true
    text sellers_h "Fishers" #2f6b8f x 95 y 372 font_size 26 hidden true
    row buyers gap 24 fit.h 165 fit.w 780 fit.x 180 fit.y 475 hidden true
    row sellers gap 24 fit.h 165 fit.w 780 fit.x 180 fit.y 290 hidden true

    card title "Where does a price come from?"

Where does a price come from, if nobody sets it?
    draw cod

On Harbourtown's quay, cod sells for much the same price at every stall each morning, and no council or boss ever wrote that number down.
    scale price bottom cites ["smith1962"] label "$ a crate" min 0 max 10 value 5 ticks 10

B: Easy. The sellers pick it: their cost, plus a bit.
    draw naive

    erase cod naive

That's most people's first answer. In January 1956  (@draw smith@)a young economist at Purdue, Vernon Smith, put it to a test in his classroom.

    erase smith

He gave each student a secret card: a buyer's card said the most they could (@draw sellers_h s1@) pay, a seller's card the least they could accept.
    draw buyers_h b1

Here is a small version on the quay: say, five buyers who would pay at most eight, seven, six, five or four dollars a crate.
    draw b2 b3 b4 b5

B: And five fishers who won't sell below two, three, four, five or six.
    draw s2 s3 s4 s5

Nobody sees anyone else's card: people shout bids and offers, and anyone may take one.
    highlight buyers sellers effect pulse

    ask "Where will the trading price settle? Click the price line." on price store g
        right: Close: you said {g} dollars, and it settles at {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g} dollars; it settles at {g.true}. A guess is a fine place to start.

In Smith's markets, the prices drifted toward one number within a few rounds of trading.
    highlight price_answer effect pulse

B: So a price nobody chose still lands somewhere precise, and the cards decide where.
    highlight b4 s4 effect box

## Two lines that cross
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Crates a morning (Q)
    y_label: Price, $ a crate (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
    supply:
      curvature: linear
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: P* = $5
      q_label: Q* = 50
    regions:
      - consumer_surplus
      - producer_surplus
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 100]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: crates

    card title "Two lines that cross"

B: The answer to that guess is hidden in the cards, once you sort them.
    draw axes

Sort them how?
    point at.data [0,80] gesture tap

B: Line the buyers up, the most eager first; each card shows the price that buyer would still pay.
    draw demand_curve

B: With a whole quay of buyers, the steps blur into a line sloping down: the demand curve.
    draw label_D

So at each price, it says how many crates buyers would take.
    point at.data [30,70] gesture circle

B: Now the fishers, cheapest first: each card is the lowest price that fisher will accept.
    draw supply_curve

B: Their line slopes up, the supply curve: at each price, how many crates are offered.
    draw label_S

And where the two lines cross, both sides want the same amount: say, fifty crates at five dollars.
    draw equilibrium_point guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar

    pause 0.5

B: Economists call that point the equilibrium: a place where nothing pushes the price to change.
    draw label_E

B: Every buyer left of the crossing values a crate above five dollars,  (@draw ps_region@)an (@hide label_CS label_PS@)d every fisher there can land one for less.
    draw cs_region

So five dollars is no one's choice: it's the one price where every willing buyer meets a willing seller.
    highlight equilibrium_point effect pulse

## The push back
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Crates a morning (Q)
    y_label: Price, $ a crate (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.896
    supply:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.958
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: false
      p_label: $5
      q_label: '50'
    price_floor:
      level: 70
      label: Price
      show_surplus: true
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 100]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: crates
    price_ceiling:
      level: 96
      label: Price
      show_shortage: true

    text n30 "30" at.data [25,6] font_size 24 hidden true
    text n70 "70" at.data [75,6] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "The push back"

B: A crossing only matters if the market can find it. What if the price starts somewhere wrong?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E

Let's try. Say the fishers open at seven dollars a crate.
    draw floor_line label_floor

At seven, fishers bring seventy crates, but buyers want only thirty.
    draw surplus_guide_qd label_Qd surplus_guide_qs label_Qs n30 n70

B: So crates are still on the quay at noon. A surplus, and fish doesn't keep.
    draw surplus_arrow label_surplus

    quiz "Crates are left over at $7. Which way will the price move?" voice b
        right: Down: a fisher stuck with cod would rather sell cheaper than not at all.
        wrong: Picture a fisher at noon with unsold fish. What would she do?
        + Down
        * Up
        * It stays at $7

    erase n30 n70

Right: a fisher with unsold cod undercuts, and each cut sells a few more crates and lands a few fewer.
    animate price_floor.level 58 duration 4

The surplus shrinks until, at five dollars, it's gone.
    animate price_floor.level 50 duration 2.5

    erase surplus_guide_qd surplus_guide_qs label_Qd label_Qs surplus_arrow label_surplus label_floor

    erase floor_line

B: Then the other wa (@draw ceiling_line label_ceiling@)y: say the price starts at three dollars.
    animate price_ceiling.level 30 duration 2

Buyers want about seventy crates and fishers bring about thirty: a shortage, and some buyers go home empty-handed.
    draw n30 n70

B: And they don't just give up, they bid mor (@erase n30 n70@)e, so the price climbs back toward five.
    animate price_ceiling.level 50 duration 4

    show floor_line

Pause here and drag the price line yourself: put it anywhere but five, and see which gap opens to push it back.
    explore

B: A surplus pushes the price down, a shortage pushes it up, so it keeps getting shoved toward the crossing.
    highlight equilibrium_point effect pulse

## Does every market find it?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Smith would hand you a buyer's card.
    good: You read a gap like a fishmonger.
    poor: Watch which side is left waiting.
    none: The quay will still be there tomorrow.
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Crates (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.896
    supply:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.958
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: $5
      q_label: '50'
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 100]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: crates
