      x: 30
      'y': 130
      w: 500
      h: 520
sources:
    - id: chamberlin1948
      title: An Experimental Imperfect Market
      authors: Edward H. Chamberlin
      year: 1948
      finding: In a Harvard classroom market where students haggled privately in pairs, prices did not settle at the competitive equilibrium and more units were traded than it predicts.
    - id: smith1962
      title: An Experimental Study of Competitive Market Behavior
      authors: Vernon L. Smith
      year: 1962
      finding: With every bid and offer called out publicly (a double auction), inexperienced traders converged quickly to the competitive equilibrium.

    portrait chamberlin of "Edward Hastings Chamberlin" cameo true hidden true
    box ch "Haggling in pairs, 1948" width 340 height 150 radius 10 icon handshake style.stroke #b5482e in pair hidden true
    box sm "Open shouting, 1956" width 340 height 150 radius 10 icon megaphone style.stroke #2f6b8f in pair hidden true
    column pair gap 90 fit.h 440 fit.w 400 fit.x 560 fit.y 200 hidden true
    text ch_v "missed the crossing" below ch gap 14 #b5482e cites ["chamberlin1948"] font_size 26 hidden true
    text sm_v "found it" below sm gap 14 #2f6b8f cites ["smith1962"] font_size 26 hidden true
    scratch sure work ["Lab auctions: consensus","Real markets, how fast: contested"] x 760 y 450 font_size 26 hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"Q_d = 100 - 10P"},{"tex":"Q_s = 10P"},{"tex":"100 - 10P = 10P"},{"tex":"P = 5,\\ Q = 50"}] x 760 y 440 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards cases bins ["Shortage","Surplus"] check each hidden true
        Surplus: Winter coats unsold in March
        Shortage: Queue round the block for a new phone
        Surplus: Strawberries left at closing
        Shortage: Long waiting list for flats

    card title "Does every market find it?"

B: But hold on. Does every market find the crossing, or only Smith's classroom?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

Smith wondered that too: as a Harv (@draw chamberlin@)ard student he had sat in Edward Chamberlin's classroom market.

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar to 0.35

    erase chamberlin

There, buyers and sellers wandered the room and haggled in pairs, each deal private.
    draw ch

Prices never settled at the crossing, and more was traded than the curves predict.
    draw ch_v

B: So what did Smith change?
    draw sm

One thing: every bid and offer was shouted fo (@draw sm_v@)r all to hear, and then the market found it.

B: So the cards decide where the price is, but the market's rules decide whether it's found.
    highlight ch sm effect box

    erase pair ch_v sm_v

That open auctions find the crossing has been repeated in lab after lab since: consensus.
    draw sure_box sure_line_1

B: How fast real markets get there, with printed menus and long contracts, is still contested.
    draw sure_line_2

    pause 0.5

    erase sure

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math_box"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test

    ask "Find the crossing with algebra: open the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math_box

    erase mathq

Say buyers want a hundred crates minus ten for every dollar of price, and fishers offer ten crates per dollar.
    draw math_box math_line_1 math_line_2

B: The crossing is where the two are equal: twenty P is a hundred, so the price is five and fifty crates change hands.
    draw math_line_3 math_line_4

    pause 0.5

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    quiz "Concert tickets sell out in minutes and are resold for three times the price. What does that tell you about the original price?"
        intro: Three new cases to finish.
        right: Below: at that price far more people wanted tickets than there were seats, a shortage, and resellers charge what the gap will bear.
        wrong: Sold out in minutes means more wanted than offered. Which gap is that?
        + It was below the equilibrium
        * It was above the equilibrium
        * It was at the equilibrium

    hide axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

    draw cases

    ask "Shortage or surplus? Sort each case." on cases store f stage own voice b
        right: All four. Things left over mean the price is too high; people left waiting mean it is too low.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Left over is a surplus; left waiting is a shortage.

    erase cases

    show axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

    quiz "Where would you expect the price of the same old lamp to vary more from one sale to the next?"
        right: The flea market: private haggling is Chamberlin's market, where prices never settle on one number.
        wrong: Think about who hears the other offers.
        + A flea market where each buyer haggles alone
        * An auction room where everyone hears every bid
        * No difference

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar to 1

Nobody sets the price: everyone's cards set it together, and a mar (@highlight equilibrium_point effect pulse@)ket that lets them hear each other finds it.
    animate box full duration 2.5

